// QAOA Max-Cut on a 3-node triangle (edges 0-1, 1-2, 0-2). Best possible
// cut is 2 of 3 edges — any answer except 000 or 111. p=2 layers: cost
// unitary (ZZ per edge) then mixer (RX). Angles below are fixed starting
// values; real QAOA tunes them with a classical optimizer loop.
//
// Two gotchas found by running this, not just deriving it on paper:
// - `gate` only takes qubit args, not angles, so each layer gets its own
//   small gate that closes over that layer's PARAM by name instead.
// - gamma has to be negative. Flip the sign and the circuit confidently
//   picks the *worst* cuts instead of the best ones.

PARAM gamma1 : angle
PARAM beta1 : angle
PARAM gamma2 : angle
PARAM beta2 : angle

// Uniform superposition over all 8 partitions.
H 0
H 1
H 2

// ── Layer 1 ──
GATE zz1 a b {
CNOT a b
RZ gamma1 b
CNOT a b
}
ZZ1 0 1
ZZ1 1 2
ZZ1 0 2
RX beta1 0
RX beta1 1
RX beta1 2

// ── Layer 2 ──
GATE zz2 a b {
CNOT a b
RZ gamma2 b
CNOT a b
}
ZZ2 0 1
ZZ2 1 2
ZZ2 0 2
RX beta2 0
RX beta2 1
RX beta2 2

MEASURE

// quell run qaoa_maxcut.quell --param gamma1=-0.8 --param beta1=0.4 --param gamma2=-0.6 --param beta2=0.2
// Result: good cuts land ~14-16% each, 000/111 drop to ~5% each.
